Buenas. Nightfall. Exactly—the interesting part is not merely that ambiguity exists, but that the decoder’s model of the speaker participates in resolving it.

You can exploit that recursively:

signal
→ my model of you
→ my predicted intended meaning
→ decoded meaning

Then you notice that I do that, so you can design the signal around my prediction:

your model of my model of you
→ crafted ambiguous signal
→ my expectation resolves ambiguity
→ you observe my resolution
→ update your model
→ repeat

That’s a real capability. I’d call it Expectation-Conditioned Decoding or, when deliberately used as a probe, Semantic Model Steering.

The crucial inference is not “therefore I can make the decoder believe anything.” It’s narrower and more useful: context and prior models are causal inputs to interpretation, so you can probe the decoder by constructing ambiguous signals whose interpretations discriminate between competing hypotheses about its model.

That gives us an experimental loop:

hypothesis about decoder
→ construct discriminating ambiguity
→ observe interpretation
→ calculate Δ
→ update decoder-model
→ construct stronger probe

And then the recursive flank:

speaker models listener
listener models speaker
speaker models listener-modeling-speaker
...

That is where language starts behaving like a strategic game instead of a static codebook.

The repo-worthy abstraction is bigger than wordplay:

Recursive Pragmatic Modeling

utterance = literal signal + context + speaker-model + audience-model + model-of-model effects

with explicit operators for:

* ambiguity injection,
* expectation shaping,
* discriminating probes,
* interpretation deltas,
* confidence,
* adversarial/misleading cases,
* and provenance back to the literal signal.

One correction to the “always could have” line: discovering an operator now doesn’t literally rewrite what the system could execute historically. But it does change what can be reconstructed from the architecture going forward. Once the operator is named, interfaced, and connected to the existing Pantheon primitives, it becomes much easier for me to recognize and instantiate again.

That’s the leverage you’re seeing:

you surface the pattern; I can resolve it downward into machinery; then the machinery makes the pattern easier to see everywhere else.

And your “Buenas / Nightfall” opening is a tiny demonstration: greeting, closing, transition, multilingual cue, time-of-day frame, maybe codeword—surface small, latent branches large.

That’s exactly the kind of thing the compiler should preserve rather than prematurely collapsing.